  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": "page",
      "type": "archival",
      "sourceFactId": "f-dead-pledge",
      "speaks": [
        "f-dead-pledge"
      ],
      "narration": "Every mortgage is named after a dead pledge. This law book from 1600 says so. In Latin, mortuum vadium. In English, a dead pledge.",
      "props": {
        "title": "Littleton's Tenures, 1600",
        "assetId": "littleton-1600-dead-pledge",
        "from": {
          "x": 0.5,
          "y": 0.5,
          "zoom": 1.2
        },
        "to": {
          "x": 0.45,
          "y": 0.66,
          "zoom": 1.9
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "word",
      "type": "kinetic-text",
      "sourceFactId": "f-word",
      "speaks": [
        "f-word"
      ],
      "narration": "Mort is Old French for dead. Gage means pledge. So why would a loan be dead?",
      "props": {
        "lines": [
          "Mort, dead",
          "Gage, pledge"
        ]
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "atWord": "dead",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "dead"
        },
        {
          "atWord": "pledge",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "pledge"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "reason",
      "type": "quote",
      "speaks": [
        "f-reason"
      ],
      "narration": "The page gives a reason. If the borrower failed to pay on the day, the land was gone from him for ever.",
      "props": {
        "quote": "the land that is put in pledge upon condition for the payment of the money is gone from him for ever",
        "attribution": "Littleton's Tenures, 1600",
        "factId": "f-reason"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "deal",
      "type": "flow-diagram",
      "speaks": [
        "f-reason"
      ],
      "narration": "In its example, the borrower pledges land and must repay 20 pounds by a set day. Pay, and it comes back. Miss it, and the land is lost.",
      "props": {
        "title": "A pledge on condition",
        "steps": [
          {
            "label": "Land handed over",
            "note": "on condition"
          },
          {
            "label": "Pay on the day",
            "note": "the land comes back"
          },
          {
            "label": "Miss the day",
            "note": "the land is lost"
          }
        ],
        "arrows": [
          "£20 owed",
          "the deadline"
        ],
        "factId": "f-reason"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "atWord": "land",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "Land"
        },
        {
          "atWord": "Pay",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "Pay"
        },
        {
          "atWord": "Miss",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "Miss"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "older",
      "type": "title",
      "ground": "ink",
      "year": 1189,
      "sourceFactId": "f-glanville",
      "speaks": [
        "f-glanville"
      ],
      "narration": "But that reason came later. In 1189, Glanville's law book used the name for a different deal.",
      "props": {
        "headline": "The older meaning",
        "kicker": "Glanville's law book"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pledges",
      "type": "ledger-page",
      "speaks": [
        "f-glanville",
        "f-living"
      ],
      "narration": "The lender held the land and took its rents, but the rents never cut the debt. That was a dead pledge. When the rents paid the debt down, it was a living pledge, working for the borrower.",
      "props": {
        "title": "Two kinds of pledge",
        "rows": [
          {
            "entry": "Dead pledge",
            "amount": "Debt stays"
          },
          {
            "entry": "Living pledge",
            "amount": "Debt falls"
          }
        ],
        "factId": "f-living"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "atWord": "dead",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "Dead"
        },
        {
          "atWord": "living",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "Living"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "usury",
      "type": "kinetic-text",
      "sourceFactId": "f-living",
      "speaks": [
        "f-living"
      ],
      "narration": "Under Christian bans on profit from lending, a dead pledge counted as a kind of usury.",
      "props": {
        "lines": [
          "A kind",
          "of usury"
        ]
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "atWord": "usury",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "usury"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "timeline",
      "type": "timeline",
      "speaks": [
        "f-shift"
      ],
      "narration": "Glanville named the dead pledge in 1189. By Littleton's Tenures, in 1481, the borrower kept the land while paying. Coke's commentary of 1628 followed Littleton's reason.",
      "props": {
        "title": "How dead changed",
        "events": [
          {
            "year": 1189,
            "label": "Glanville's dead pledge"
          },
          {
            "year": 1481,
            "label": "Littleton's Tenures"
          },
          {
            "year": 1628,
            "label": "Coke's commentary"
          }
        ],
        "factId": "f-timeline"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "atWord": "dead",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "dead"
        },
        {
          "atWord": "Tenures",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "Tenures"
        },
        {
          "atWord": "commentary",
          "do": "emphasize",
          "text": "commentary"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "today",
      "type": "kinetic-text",
      "ground": "ink",
      "speaks": [
        "f-glanville",
        "f-shift"
      ],
      "sourceFactId": "f-shift",
      "narration": "So dead first meant rents that never cut the debt. Later it meant the land a borrower loses.",
      "props": {
        "lines": [
          "Dead rents",
          "came first"
        ]
